// Document is a paper the deal depends on, proved to exist and proved not to
// have changed.
//
// # The chain holds the hash and never the document
//
// A subscription agreement names its investor and their cheque; a diligence
// pack contains a private company's accounts. Writing either to a public chain
// is a permanent disclosure that no later correction can withdraw, and it would
// be made on behalf of people who did not consent to it. So what is stored here
// is a SHA-256 digest, a kind, a title and a location - never bytes.
//
// What that is worth is precise, and worth being precise about. It proves that
// a document with exactly these contents existed at a stated block, attested by
// a named operator group whose members can be asked about it. It proves that a
// file produced in a dispute years later either is or is not that document. It
// proves nothing whatever about whether the document says what its title
// claims, whether anybody signed it, or whether it is still available - the URI
// can rot, and an operator who takes the file down has removed the document
// while leaving this record standing. A hash is evidence of integrity, not of
// custody and not of truth.
//
// # Append-only, and superseded rather than replaced
//
// Documents are never deleted and never edited. A register of what was agreed
// that can forget what was agreed is not a register. An amended agreement is a
// NEW document naming the one it supersedes, so the chain of amendments reads
// in both directions and the superseded version stays visible - which is the
// version somebody will need, because a dispute is almost always about what the
// terms were at a date rather than about what they are now.
